              "message": "Templates: the code gate holds against the independent review\n\nA trust policy decides whose approvals count. chap-trust.json, written by\ntrust.mjs from the workspace and committed in the governed repository,\npins the reviewers' and agents' keys and the rule; with no file, the\ngate's own records stand in, with the reviewers chap.config.json names.\nAn approval counts only from a reviewer the policy names, never from an\nagent, with its signature checked against the pinned key, and the\nagent's signed submission must be the proposed artefact. The policy's\nrule is applied to the approvals on record, whatever rule the review ran\nunder, and the commit carries one CHAP-Reviewer trailer per approver.\n\nAn approval covers one commit on the commit it was approved against.\nThe hooks and the verifier check that the desk reads the approved patch\nas the change git makes with it: the desk's own parser reads the patch,\ngit applies it, and each file git changed must be a section the desk\nshows, with the same kind of change and mode, whose hunks give the file\ngit wrote. Text before the first file, names that differ between header\nlines, a binary section for a text file and a submodule are refused, in\nthe desk as well. Patches are written with every setting that shapes the\ntext fixed, and file contents come from the trees. A merge commit fails\nunless it is a clean merge and the flag asks for that; a commit without\nan approval passes only when a person the policy lists signed it.\n\nThe pull request workflow runs on pull_request_target with the verifier\nand the policy from the base branch, reading the pull request's commits\nas data. push-notes.mjs merges and pushes the evidence notes.\n\nThe desk marks a patch it cannot show faithfully and refuses to approve\nit, shows the patch an edit makes before it is sent, offers a file for\nediting only when its contents agree with its patch, offers no edit\nunder a multi-approval rule from any control, shows mode changes, keeps\nlinks to http and paths on the server, counts every task in Insights,\nand shows the reviewer their key's fingerprint. The agent writes nothing\nunder .git or through a symbolic link, carries on after a restart with\nits change still waiting, and the hooks keep their state in the git\ndirectory, worktrees included. Both servers answer a malformed path or\nlimit with 400 and a path outside the analytics pages with 404.",
