              "message": "Templates: the code gate holds against the second review\n\nApprovals name their round. The desk sends, with each decision, the\ndigest of the submission that opened the review round, and the gate\ncounts an approval only in that round, so approvals from before and after\na revision no longer add up to a quorum.\n\nA pull request is checked against its base. verify.mjs --base requires\nthe range to be one line of commits from where it leaves the base's\nhistory, with no merge in it, and refuses an approval already used\nanywhere in that history, so an approved change that landed and was\nreverted cannot return on an older commit. The workflow passes the base.\n\nThe note's submission and decisions must come from the workspace the\npolicy covers. A trust policy is checked before it is used: the rule,\nparticipant URIs (prototype names refused) and non-empty key lists.\n\nEvery git command that reads a change runs with the repository's\nattributes and configuration set aside, and binary is decided by content,\nso a .gitattributes in the change cannot hide a text change. The contents\nthe desk shows beside a patch are checked against the repository's blobs,\nand a change that lists a file twice is refused.\n\nThe hooks keep their state in the worktree's own git directory,\ncommit-msg fails closed when pre-commit recorded nothing, and pre-commit\nsays why an approved change on the same parent does not match.\n\nThe built-in agent refuses the names a file system takes for .git and any\npath inside the gate project. The desk marks characters a reader cannot\nsee in changed lines.\n\nTests for each. The README, DESIGN.md and the changelog say where the\ngate holds: the hooks are the guardrail on a developer's machine, and the\npull request check, required by branch protection, is the boundary.",
